<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Arras (1482)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-burgundian-succession">War of the Burgundian Succession</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Arras of 23 December 1482 settled the Burgundian inheritance after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a>. Concluded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> and Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> with the Estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, it betrothed Mary's daughter Margaret of Austria to the Dauphin Charles, with Artois and the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> as her dowry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> stayed with Mary's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip the Handsome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1482.jpg" alt="Treaty of Arras (1482)" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (sidonius 09:07, 5 September 2026 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte-Haus-Burgund-5-EN.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
